WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:50.000
Thank you for listening to The Rest Is History. For weekly bonus episodes, ad-free listening, early access to series, and membership of our much-loved chat community, go to therestishistory.com and join the club. That is therestishistory.com. Saturday 5th October 1974 was a big night for Paul Cray and Carol Burns. By coincidence, their birthdays fell on the same day, and Paul, who was due to turn 22, thought it would be nice to have a little party. Only a few weeks earlier, Carol, three years his junior, had been accepted into the Women's Royal Army Corps, so they had something special to celebrate.
感谢您收听《历史的余味》（The Rest Is History）。如需收听每周的加更节目、享受无广告体验、抢先观看系列节目并加入我们备受喜爱的聊天社区，请前往 therestishistory.com 加入会员。网址是 therestishistory.com。1974年10月5日星期六，对保罗·克雷（Paul Cray）和卡罗尔·伯恩斯（Carol Burns）来说是一个重要的夜晚。巧合的是，他们的生日在同一天，即将满22岁的保罗觉得举办一个小派对会很不错。就在几周前，比他小三岁的卡罗尔刚被女子皇家陆军军团（Women's Royal Army Corps）录取，所以他们有特别的理由庆祝。

2
00:00:50.000 --> 00:01:30.000
She chose the venue, a pub not far from her barracks, recommended by some of her new friends who used to meet their boyfriends there on Saturday nights. Meanwhile, Paul had a surprise up his sleeve. Unbeknown to Carol, he had invited her parents down from Boreham Wood for the evening, and when she left headquarters, all three of them were waiting outside to greet her. They walked on to the pub, the Horse and Groom in Guildford, which was already filling up. The atmosphere was noisy, happy. Many of the drinkers were soldiers and their girlfriends, enjoying a night out. The Burns family found a table in an alcove by the jukebox. Carol disappeared to the toilet for a minute or two.
她选择了地点，那是离她军营不远的一家酒吧，是她的一些新朋友推荐的，她们过去常在周六晚上在那里见男朋友。与此同时，保罗还准备了一个惊喜。卡罗尔不知道，他邀请了她的父母从博勒姆伍德（Boreham Wood）赶来参加晚会。当她离开总部时，他们三个人都在外面等着迎接她。他们步行前往吉尔福德（Guildford）的“马与新郎”（Horse and Groom）酒吧，那里已经挤满了人。气氛嘈杂而欢乐。许多酒客都是士兵和他们的女朋友，正在享受夜晚的时光。伯恩斯一家在点唱机旁的一个凹室里找到了座位。卡罗尔消失了一两分钟去上厕所。

3
00:01:30.000 --> 00:02:10.000
When she came back, Paul had moved up and taken her seat, so she slipped in beside him. It was almost nine o'clock. Then, quite suddenly, there was a gigantic bang and everything went black. The next thing Carol knew, she was lying on the ground, her ears full of buzzing, her mouth full of smoke and dust. Someone was lying on the floor beside me, she said later. It was Paul. So Dominic, that is from your history of Britain in 1974 to 1979, Seasons in the Sun. And it describes what has been commemorated as the Guildford Pub bombing.
当她回来时，保罗已经挪动位置坐了她的位子，所以她滑到他身边坐下。当时快九点了。然后，突然之间，一声巨响，一切都陷入了黑暗。卡罗尔醒来时，发现自己躺在地上，耳朵里嗡嗡作响，嘴里满是烟尘。她后来回忆说，有人躺在我旁边的地板上。那是保罗。那么，多米尼克，这段内容出自你关于1974年至1979年英国历史的著作《太阳下的季节》（Seasons in the Sun）。它描述了后来被称为“吉尔福德酒吧爆炸案”的纪念事件。

4
00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:41.000
So Paul is dead? Yes. Paul was killed straight away. He's one of five people killed that evening, 5th of October, 1974. There was a bomb in the alcove that you mentioned by the jukebox that had been planted by the Provisional IRA. It's a reminder of how chance matters in history because when Carol went to the toilet, they moved places. If she was still sitting where she had been, she would have been killed, not Paul. Her parents were very badly injured. Her father, I think, was in a coma for five weeks.
所以保罗死了？是的。保罗当场遇难。他是1974年10月5日那天晚上遇难的五人之一。你提到的点唱机旁边的凹室里有一枚炸弹，是临时派（Provisional IRA）安放的。这提醒了我们偶然性在历史中有多重要，因为当卡罗尔去上厕所时，他们换了位置。如果她还坐在原来的地方，死的就是她，而不是保罗了。她的父母受了重伤。我想她的父亲昏迷了五周。

5
00:02:41.000 --> 00:03:29.000
Four other people were killed, 68 people injured. Two of the people who died were teenagers, teenage girls who'd also joined the Women's Royal Army Corps, and two teenage boys, Scots Guards, Sir William Forsyth and James Hunter, who were friends from the same street in Renfrewshire, who had only joined up a month beforehand. And the Guildford pub bombing sent an absolute shockwave through Britain. There have been many bombings, of course, in Northern Ireland in the last couple of years, but this was seen as something, a bomb in a pub in Britain, in the home counties, was seen as unbelievably shocking. And of course, what made it more shocking, Tom, was that afterwards, the police charged four innocent people
另有四人死亡，68人受伤。死者中有两名十几岁的女孩，她们也加入了女子皇家陆军军团；还有两名十几岁的男孩，是苏格兰卫队的威廉·福赛斯爵士（Sir William Forsyth）和詹姆斯·亨特（James Hunter），他们是来自伦弗鲁郡（Renfrewshire）同一条街的朋友，才入伍一个月。吉尔福德酒吧爆炸案在英国引起了巨大的震动。当然，过去几年北爱尔兰发生过许多爆炸案，但这被视为一件极其令人震惊的事——在英国本土、在伦敦周边郡县的酒吧里发生爆炸。当然，汤姆，更令人震惊的是，事后警方指控了四名无辜者，

6
00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:49.000
who went to prison for 15 years. Yeah, because that's what it's chiefly remembered for now, isn't it? Yes. And almost certainly the culprits were a group called the Balcombe Street Gang, who were a provisional IRA unit who were kind of on the loose in England in 1974 and 1975, and who actually said at their trial in 1977,
他们坐了15年的牢。是的，因为这正是现在人们主要记住它的原因，不是吗？是的。而且几乎可以肯定，罪魁祸首是一个叫“巴尔科姆街帮”（Balcombe Street Gang）的组织，他们是临时派爱尔兰共和军的一个小组，1974年和1975年在英国流窜作案，他们实际上在1977年的审判中承认，

7
00:03:49.000 --> 00:04:42.000
there were innocent people in prison for the Guildford pub bombing, but the police and someone didn't listen. So yeah, it's a pretty shocking story. And we're at the final episode of this 1974 marathon, and this is five days before the general election campaign, the second general election in 1974, which is where we ended, didn't we, last time? Harold Wilson has decided on the showdown with Ted Heath to try and give himself the mandate, because he's been leading a minority government. And this sort of darkness overshadows the election. And you described in the previous episode how Wilson is seen by many people on the right as so illegitimate as actually to be potentially a Soviet mole. People on the left are talking with some justification of the possibility of a right-wing coup.
吉尔福德酒吧爆炸案中有无辜者入狱，但警方和某些人并没有听。所以，这是一个相当令人震惊的故事。我们现在是1974年马拉松系列的最后一集，距离大选竞选还有五天，这是1974年的第二次大选，也就是我们上次结束的地方，对吧？哈罗德·威尔逊（Harold Wilson）决定与泰德·希思（Ted Heath）进行决战，试图为自己争取授权，因为他一直领导着一个少数派政府。这种黑暗笼罩着选举。你在上一集中描述了威尔逊在右翼许多人眼中是多么不合法，甚至可能是一个苏联间谍。左翼人士则有理有据地谈论着右翼政变的可能性。

8
00:04:42.000 --> 00:05:21.000
Yes. So there is a sense of extreme polarisation in the country. And then to have this act of brutal violence, as you say, in the home counties, in Surrey, one of the richest and most prosperous areas in Britain. I assume then that it must have radically enhanced the sense that many people have that this is a state of crisis like none that Britain has faced since the war? I think what it doesn't do is to create a sort of what you might call a histrionic or extreme reaction. Actually, what it adds to is a sense, I think, of weariness, of depression. Because don't forget, the conflict in Northern
是的。所以全国范围内有一种极端的两极分化感。然后发生了这种残酷的暴力行为，正如你所说，在伦敦周边郡县，在萨里郡（Surrey），这是英国最富裕、最繁荣的地区之一。我假设这一定从根本上加剧了许多人的感觉，即这是一个自战争以来英国从未面临过的危机状态？我认为它并没有制造出一种所谓的夸张或极端的反应。实际上，我认为它增加了一种疲惫感和压抑感。因为别忘了，北爱尔兰的冲突

9
00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:43.000
Ireland has been going on since the late 1960s. So we're into kind of year five a bit now. And most people don't understand it. Most people in Great Britain don't understand it. They don't really care, to be brutally honest. But they are horrified by it and by the fact that it is, as they see it, seeping into the life of... So the Times actually says that, doesn't it? It says that no election since the war had been held in such a mood of public uncertainty
从20世纪60年代末就开始了。所以我们现在进入了第五个年头。大多数人并不理解它。英国的大多数人并不理解它。说实话，他们并不真正关心。但他们对此感到恐惧，因为正如他们所见，它正在渗透到……所以《泰晤士报》实际上是这样说的，不是吗？它说，自战争以来，没有任何一次选举是在这样一种公众不确定性

10
00:05:43.000 --> 00:06:21.000
and depression. So depression rather than anger. Yes. I mean, there is anger, as we will see later on. There are people who are calling for the death penalty for terrorists because the death penalty had been done away with just a few years earlier. But I think it adds to a sense of kind of world weariness, of shabbiness, of ineffectual government, and a sense of helplessness, actually, I guess is the word, Tom, that Britain is now, having been for so long the great actor on the world stage, that for the first time, I think, in the mid-1970s, people realised that Britain is actually on the receiving end of change and of major geopolitical developments.
和压抑的情绪中举行的。所以是压抑而不是愤怒。是的。我的意思是，确实有愤怒，我们稍后会看到。有些人呼吁对恐怖分子判处死刑，因为死刑在几年前才被废除。但我认为这增加了一种世界性的疲惫感、破败感、政府的无能，以及一种无助感，汤姆，我想这才是那个词，英国长期以来一直是世界舞台上的主角，但在20世纪70年代中期，人们第一次意识到英国实际上正处于变革和重大地缘政治发展的接收端。

11
00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:58.000
And there's a sense of impotence, I think, impotence of the government to do anything about Northern Ireland, but impotence to deal with inflation or the pace of globalisation and de-industrialisation and all of these kinds of things. And is there a sense also of, I might almost say boredom, tedium, a sense that this is just an endless cycle of two rather shop-soiled leaders in the form of Wilson and Heath? Neither of them are inspirational. Neither of them really seem to promise a kind of radical change. Yeah, I think that's absolutely right. So, Wilson, who we talked about a lot last time, the sort of chaotics, I mean, some people might think we were being a bit harsh on Wilson, that sort of chaotic six-month period after he became
而且我认为有一种无能感，政府在北爱尔兰问题上无能为力，在应对通货膨胀、全球化步伐和去工业化等问题上也无能为力。是否还有一种，我几乎可以说厌倦、乏味的感觉，觉得这只是威尔逊和希思这两个相当陈旧的领导人之间无休止的循环？他们两人都无法鼓舞人心。他们两人似乎都没有承诺某种激进的变革。是的，我认为这绝对正确。所以，威尔逊，我们上次谈了很多，那种混乱的六个月时期，在他成为

12
